course: dictionary
lesson: 104
title: ADHD: a difference, not a fault
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: ADHD is a word you may hear at school or at home. It stands for attention deficit hyperactivity disorder. It is a difference in how the brain works, and it can make it hard to pay attention, sit still or wait your turn.
explain: Context helps you understand it. «ADHD makes sitting still and focusing hard, and it is not naughtiness either.» The words «not naughtiness» tell you that ADHD is not bad behaviour, and the sentence says it is not the child's fault.
explain: ADHD can also bring lots of energy and creativity. Teachers and families help with short tasks, movement breaks, lists and reminders. Remember that ADHD has no plural: you write «ADHD», never «ADHDs».
fact: ADHD stands for attention deficit hyperactivity disorder.
fact: ADHD is a difference in how the brain works, not laziness or naughtiness.
fact: «Many classmates live with long-term conditions, epilepsy, cystic fibrosis, ADHD, heart problems, and none of them is catching or anyone's fault.»
fact: Two useful phrases are «have ADHD» and «be diagnosed with ADHD».
fact: ADHD has no plural, so you never write «ADHDs».
exercise: What does ADHD stand for? | all day happy dogs | a very hard maths test | a type of school bus | *attention deficit hyperactivity disorder
exercise: «He's not being naughty, he's got ADHD, he just can't sit still sometimes.» What does this tell you? | *ADHD can make sitting still hard, and it is not naughtiness | ADHD is caught from other children | ADHD means a child is always naughty | ADHD is a kind of food
exercise: Why is ADHD described as «nobody's fault»? | Because it only happens on Fridays | Because teachers caused it | *Because it is a difference in the brain, so no one should blame the child | Because the child chose to have it
test: Which help is best for a child with ADHD? | Telling them they are lazy | *Short tasks, movement breaks and reminders | Taking away all their lists | Making them sit still for an hour with no break
test: Which sentence uses ADHD correctly? | Many classmates ADHD. | Many classmates are ADHDs. | *Many classmates have ADHD. | Many classmates have ADHDs.
exam: Jayden says, «ADHD is when you are just naughty.» What should you say back? | ADHD only happens to children who are rude | ADHD is just a bad habit you can stop if you try | ADHD means you cannot have any energy at all | *ADHD is a difference in how the brain works, and it is not naughtiness
